[; Blog post in plain element markup: headings, inline markup, code blocks,
   lists, blockquote, link. All v0.8.0 — no markdown surface. ]

[article
  [h1 CX in Practice: A Field Guide]

  [p By [a href=https://github.com/ardec Erik Paulson] · [date 2026-04-20]]

  [p CX is a [strong structured] format that looks like brackets and reads
    like natural text. This post walks through a typical working session.]

  [h2 Setting Up]

  [p Build from source — the whole toolchain compiles with a single command:]

  [pre [code lang=bash [#
git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx
make build   # builds libcx and the cx CLI
make test    # runs all language binding test suites
#]]]

  [h2 Your First Document]

  [p Start with a simple config. Sized type annotations pin numeric ranges,
     boolean attributes are explicit, and line comments document intent
     inline:]

  [pre [code lang=cx [#
[server host=localhost tls=true debug=false
  port::u16=8080                    # u16 covers 0..65535
  max_request_bytes::u32=1_048_576  # 1 MiB - underscores improve readability
  [tags ::string[] web api internal]
]
#]]]

  [h2 Converting Formats]

  [p Pipe any CX file through the [code cx] CLI or call the library directly:]

  [ul
    [li [code cx --json config.cx] — compact JSON for APIs]
    [li [code cx --xml  config.cx] — XML for legacy systems]
    [li [code cx --toml config.cx] — TOML for human editors]
    [li [code cx --yaml config.cx] — YAML for human editors]
  ]

  [h2 Mixed Content]

  [p CX handles documents and data in the same syntax. Inline markup works
    anywhere: [strong bold], [em italic], [code code], [del strikethrough].]

  [blockquote [p The goal was one format you never have to escape from.]]

  [h2 What's Next]

  [p See the [a href=https://cxhome.org/docs full documentation] for
    language binding guides, the conformance suite, and the grammar spec.]
]
